  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Randy V\u00e1squez bought them control. The lineup converted just enough to hold it.",
    "what_this_means": "V\u00e1squez threw seven innings of two-hit baseball on a night when the offense didn't need to carry weight. Nine hits, two multi-run innings, and the kind of balanced production that doesn't require a star turn. The Twins answered with two runs in the fourth, tying a game the Padres seized in the first, but a four-run burst in the fifth broke it open for good. Bradgley Rodriguez and Mason Miller closed without incident, combining for two scoreless innings and four strikeouts. This wasn't a margin-squeaker where one decision mattered. This was pitching that set the terms and offense that honored them. When the starter does his job this cleanly, the lineup's job narrows to one thing: don't waste it. They didn't.",
    "what_to_watch": "They are stabilizing around what works. Pitching first, enough offense to not embarrass it. V\u00e1squez's line (7 IP, 2 H, 2 ER, 3 K) is the floor they can count on. They built a four-run cushion and never relinquished it, leading from the second inning onward. The missed opportunities in the third and seventh stung only on a box-score read; in the game itself, the margin was never under threat. This is how wins look when the starter dominates and the bullpen doesn't give it back. No desperation, no late scramble, no one player carrying the entire load. It's simpler than that. Balanced pitching, timely hitting, and the bullpen holding.",
